<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karlsruhe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1715">1715</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karlsruhe is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1715 by Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-william-margrave-of-baden-durlach">Charles III William</a> of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> as a new residence. It was planned in a fan shape, with streets radiating from the palace tower. It was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> until 1945 and is now the seat of Germany's Federal Constitutional Court and Federal Court of Justice.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karlsruhe lies on the right bank of the Upper Rhine, near the French border, between the Mannheim-Ludwigshafen conurbation to the north and Strasbourg to the south. It stands almost wholly on the Upper Rhine Plain, with the Turmberg to the east and the borders of the Kraichgau, which leads to the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>. Its climate is oceanic and its winters are milder than those of most German cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to legend, the name Karlsruhe, meaning Charles's repose or Charles's peace, was given when Margrave Charles III William of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> woke from a dream in which he saw himself founding a new city, after a hunting trip. A variation says that he built the new palace to find peace from his wife. The palace was built in 1715, and a settlement grew around it, for which a mayor was appointed from 1718. From 1812 the mayors bore the title of Lord Mayor.</p><p>Karlsruhe was the capital of the Margraviate of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> from 1718 to 1771, of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> from 1771 to 1803, of the Electorate of Baden from 1803 to 1806, of the Grand Duchy of Baden from 1806 to 1918 and of the Republic of Baden from 1918 to 1945. It became a major city in the 19th century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> settled there soon after its founding, attracted by the privileges the founder offered to settlers without distinction of creed, and several Jewish families are recorded in 1717. On 9 to 10 November 1938 one synagogue was burned and another damaged, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> began on 22 October 1940, when 893 Jews were sent to the Gurs camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Of the 1,280 Jews deported directly from Karlsruhe, 1,175 perished. A new community formed after the war and built a synagogue in 1971.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and Württemberg were merged, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> became the capital of the new state. In compensation Karlsruhe applied to become the seat of the highest courts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Karlsruhe is the third-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, with about 310,000 inhabitants, and the seat of the Federal Constitutional Court and the Federal Court of Justice. The palace houses the Baden State Museum. The Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, which grew from the University of Karlsruhe, is the oldest technical university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and the Centre for Art and Media (ZKM) opened in a former weapons factory in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karlsruhe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Universities at Heidelberg and Freiburg and industry at Mannheim and <b>Karlsruhe</b> flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles III William of Baden-Durlach founded <b>Karlsruhe</b> in 1715, a planned city radiating from the palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles III William founded <b>Karlsruhe</b> in 1715 as a new residence laid out in a fan around the palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Margraviate of Baden-Durlach</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1918 a provisional government was formed in <b>Karlsruhe</b> and Grand Duke Frederick II abdicated.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-baden">Republic of Baden</a></li></ul></section>
